Entailment witnesses along a finite set of function tokens can be combined, as needed for transitivity. If v is consistent, a consistent set u' entails every input of a finite set s, and each token of s has a subset of v witnessing its entailment, then some subset of v has inputs entailed by u' and output union entailing the output union of s.

The argument is by induction on s. The empty set is witnessed by the empty subset of v. When a token q is inserted, the inductive hypothesis and the hypothesis for q supply two subsets of v, and their union remains contained in v. Inputs from the first subset are entailed by u' as given, and inputs from the second are entailed by the input of q and hence by u'. The input unions of the two subsets are consistent because their inputs are entailed by consistent sets, so consistency of v makes the output unions consistent, and the union of those output unions is the output union of the combined subset. The two output entailments therefore combine into an entailment of the output union of the enlarged set.
